Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Note Keeps Walking After the Bow Stops
- Story & Context: The debut begins at 118 BPM with one nyckelharpa note and no drums. Elin bows a resonant Dorian fifth and stops abruptly, leaving the sympathetic strings to ring in the tunnel. Sibusiso waits until that halo is nearly gone before placing the first low kick. On the next cycle, he puts the kick earlier, cutting across the resonance. The entire track grows from this alternation between exposed tail and interrupted tail.

Linnea enters with one damped floor-tom hit every second four-bar phrase, each replacing a programmed low percussion event. Thabo plays a breath-heavy bass-clarinet semitone only after the longest sympathetic decay has finished. Johan remains silent for the first minute, then adds one muted root note at the end of each eight-bar section.

At the midpoint, Elin alternates two bowed pitches: one excites a large field of sympathetic strings, the other remains comparatively dead. Sibusiso uses the bright-resonant pitch as a signal to remove drums and the dry pitch as a signal to increase them. The track ends with the main string stopped while the sympathetic field continues alone for several seconds.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty underground station tunnel after service hours, a nyckelharpa bow lifted above the string while faint vibrating sympathetic strings remain visible in macro focus, maintenance floor panels stretching into darkness, one amber lamp reflected in steel. No full lyrics; optional spoken fragments limited to “bow stops,” “note keeps walking,” “leave the tail,” and “do not fill the floor.”

Track 2: Three Steps Across a False Panel
- Story & Context: Sibusiso builds the EP’s most dance-forward groove at 122 BPM from a three-step kick pattern with one deliberately missing low hit every second bar. Linnea answers that vacancy with a dead prepared-floor-tom stroke, but only on alternating cycles. The listener therefore hears three possible versions of the same rhythmic space: electronic kick, acoustic tom, or silence.

Elin plays a four-note nyckelharpa phrase whose first and fourth notes strongly excite sympathetic strings while the middle notes stay dry. Johan reinforces only the fourth note with a short muted bass octave. Thabo uses low key-click patterns instead of pitched notes for the first half, gradually introducing breath tone as the arrangement darkens.

During the central break, all electronic drums disappear for eight bars. The groove survives through floor tom, tangent clicks, bass key noise, and the repeated resonance pattern of Elin’s phrase. When the machine beat returns, it enters without fill and lands one beat later than expected.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead view of temporary plywood maintenance flooring marked with three numbered step positions, the middle panel slightly lifted, nyckelharpa keys, floor-tom mallet, and transit engineering chalk marks surrounding it. Spoken fragments limited to “three steps,” “second panel hollow,” “kick or wood,” and “leave one missing.”

Track 3: Sympathetic String Hears the Last Train First
- Story & Context: The closer begins with distant field audio of a final empty train moving through another platform. Elin holds one low nyckelharpa drone while the sympathetic strings pick up faint harmonic vibration from the tunnel before the direct train sound reaches the recording position. The band treats this physical anticipation as the song’s narrative device.

Sibusiso programs the sparsest rhythm on the EP at 114 BPM: one kick cluster, one clap, then long gaps. Johan plays only two bass notes in the first two minutes. Linnea uses mallet drags across the floor tom head to create soft friction instead of impact. Thabo introduces low bass-clarinet multiphonics only after the train has fully disappeared from the field recording.

In the final passage, the electronic beat is removed one element at a time until only one distant kick remains every two bars. Elin stops bowing but keeps both hands on the instrument while the sympathetic strings continue vibrating from the room and previous notes. The final sound is not a played note but a faint contact-mic resonance from the nyckelharpa body as the tunnel ventilation system changes speed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty underground platform seen through a service corridor, tail lights of the last train disappearing around a curve, nyckelharpa resting against a steel barrier with one contact microphone attached, pale tile and cobalt signage under low amber work light. Spoken fragments limited to “last train,” “the strings heard it first,” “no bow now,” and “leave the tunnel ringing.”

